    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. so in the ac one, is there like a specific reason why the frequency makes it easier for current to flow? i dont really get the intuition behind why a higher frequency means lower reactance. like what's actually happening inside the component?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Inside the capacitor, higher frequencies mean the voltage is changing direction much faster, giving less time for the dielectric to move surface charges to their equilibrium positions before the field reverses. This rapid \"push and pull\" prevents the capacitor from accumulating much opposite charge, effectively allowing more current to flow through for a given voltage. Think of it like pumping water through a valve that needs to seat and release; doing that action quickly (high frequency) occurs more easily than letting the valve sit still (low frequency) where it takes longer to open fully.",
      "source": "target",
