    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "ok the water valve thing helps a bit. so if the frequency is super high, the reactance basically goes to zero right? i'm just trying to wrap my head around how the capacitor handles that massive amount of charge moving back and forth so quickly. is there some kind of limit to how much energy it can handle during those rapid flips?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You are almost there, but there is an important distinction to make: even though the theoretical reactance approaches zero at infinite frequency, a real capacitor still only has a small amount of energy stored as electrostatic potential in its electric field at any instant. The limit isn't stored energy, but rather the thermal energy generated by internal resistance (ESR) and the dielectric breakdown strength of the material as charges flip back and forth too quickly for them to separate. If the frequency gets excessively high or the voltage too high, the internal resistance creates enough heat to boil dielectric fluid or cause arcing, effectively burning a hole in the capacitor.",
